WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.960 position:63%
- The Draupner gas platform was caught

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:04.380 position:63%
between two storms in the North Sea.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.420 position:63%
Conditions were becoming dangerous,

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:11.000 position:63%
so the rig manager ordered
everyone inside and just in time,

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.660 position:63%
because unknown to
them, a massive wave was

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.660 position:63%
approaching the platform.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.800 position:63%
The Draupner had been recording
data as the storm approached.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.700 position:63%
At 3:20, waves were
cresting eight meters high.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.220 position:63%
Dangerous enough to clear the deck,

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.300 position:63%
but nothing the rig couldn't handle.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.860 position:63%
Then, at exactly 3:24,

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.900 position:63%
the sensors recorded this single spike.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.400 position:63%
A crest over two times higher
at 18 and a half meters.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.880 position:63%
From trough to peak, it stood over 25

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.060 position:63%
and a half meters tall.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.560 position:63%
It towered above everything
around it, a wall

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.640 position:63%
of water rising vertically out of the sea.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:47.440 position:63%
If you'd been sitting in
a rowboat in front of it,

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.300 position:63%
it would've loomed over you as tall

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.640 position:63%
as a nine-story building.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:55.720 position:63%
A rogue wave. But that seemed impossible.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.300 position:63%
Waves like this were myths,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.300 position:63%
legends alongside mermaids and Kraken.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.600 position:63%
So, in December 2000, the
European Union launched Maxwave.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.540 position:63%
Using European Space Agency satellites,

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.080 position:63%
the team would confirm
whether these rogue waves

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.920 position:63%
existed once and for all.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.920 position:63%
For just over three weeks,
they analyzed satellite images

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.420 position:63%
of oceans from all over the globe, looking

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.660 position:63%
for any sign of a giant wave.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.080 position:63%
They didn't find one. Instead,
they found more than 10,

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.780 position:63%
all over 25 meters high.

00:01:25.641 --> 00:01:27.381 position:63%
Ships were being built on the assumption

00:01:27.381 --> 00:01:29.921 position:63%
that waves this massive were freak events,

00:01:29.921 --> 00:01:32.301 position:63%
but if they were turning
up every week, well,

00:01:32.301 --> 00:01:34.681 position:63%
then every vessel was at risk.

00:01:34.681 --> 00:01:36.341 position:63%
No one knew how rogue waves worked

00:01:36.341 --> 00:01:38.241 position:63%
or where they'd strike next.

00:01:38.241 --> 00:01:39.841 position:63%
So, in this video, we're uncovering

00:01:39.841 --> 00:01:41.661 position:63%
what goes on inside a wave.

00:01:41.661 --> 00:01:43.441 position:63%
- Oh, that's so satisfying.

00:01:43.441 --> 00:01:45.441 position:63%
- How they're built, how they fall apart.

00:01:45.441 --> 00:01:48.281 position:63%
- This is the biggest wave. Now
it breaks a little bit here.

00:01:48.281 --> 00:01:50.821 position:63%
- And whether we can make
a rogue wave of our own.

00:01:50.821 --> 00:01:52.521 position:63%
- Doesn't matter.

00:01:53.421 --> 00:01:55.661 position:63%
- Now, you might be thinking
maybe these massive waves

00:01:55.661 --> 00:01:57.341 position:63%
are just tsunamis.

00:01:57.341 --> 00:02:00.401 position:63%
I mean, a tsunami can travel
at 800 kilometers an hour,

00:02:00.401 --> 00:02:02.341 position:63%
the cruising speed of a jet,

00:02:02.341 --> 00:02:06.301 position:63%
and can stand 30 meters tall,
even detectable from space.

00:02:06.301 --> 00:02:09.041 position:63%
Except if one passed by
you in the open ocean,

00:02:09.041 --> 00:02:10.501 position:63%
you probably wouldn't notice it.

00:02:11.681 --> 00:02:14.181 position:63%
That's because a tsunami
is made when an earthquake

00:02:14.181 --> 00:02:16.901 position:63%
or a large landslide shifts the sea floor,

00:02:16.901 --> 00:02:18.361 position:63%
displacing a huge volume

00:02:18.361 --> 00:02:21.721 position:63%
of water all at once over a massive area.

00:02:21.721 --> 00:02:23.381 position:63%
That creates a very wide,

00:02:23.381 --> 00:02:27.061 position:63%
but not very tall bulge of
water, barely a meter high,

00:02:27.061 --> 00:02:29.241 position:63%
but hundreds of kilometers long.

00:02:29.241 --> 00:02:31.561 position:63%
It's only when this wave nears the coast

00:02:31.561 --> 00:02:34.301 position:63%
that the rising sea floor slows it down.

00:02:34.301 --> 00:02:35.641 position:63%
Because the wave is so long,

00:02:35.641 --> 00:02:37.761 position:63%
the front slows much
earlier than the back.

00:02:38.641 --> 00:02:41.401 position:63%
All that water has to go
somewhere so it bunches up,

00:02:41.401 --> 00:02:45.361 position:63%
growing taller and steeper
until it's a monster.

00:02:45.361 --> 00:02:48.421 position:63%
That's why in Japanese,
tsunami means harbor wave.

00:02:49.461 --> 00:02:51.562 position:63%
They only happen at the shore.

00:02:51.562 --> 00:02:52.922 position:63%
Out at sea, there's nothing to slow it

00:02:52.922 --> 00:02:54.182 position:63%
down so it doesn't grow.

00:02:55.302 --> 00:02:57.622 position:63%
Now, the Draupner platform
was neither in the deep

00:02:57.622 --> 00:02:59.602 position:63%
ocean nor at the coast.

00:02:59.602 --> 00:03:02.322 position:63%
The water beneath it was
about 70 meters deep,

00:03:02.322 --> 00:03:04.942 position:63%
so a passing tsunami
would've grown a little bit,

00:03:04.942 --> 00:03:07.482 position:63%
but only by a factor of
about two and a half.

00:03:07.482 --> 00:03:09.442 position:63%
That makes a few meters at most,

00:03:09.442 --> 00:03:11.442 position:63%
nowhere near the 25 that they measured.

00:03:12.942 --> 00:03:16.922 position:63%
So, if it wasn't a tsunami,
what had hit the Draupner?

00:03:16.922 --> 00:03:19.282 position:63%
Well, there'd been reports
of similar giant waves

00:03:19.282 --> 00:03:22.922 position:63%
for centuries, sinking boats
and smashing lighthouses,

00:03:22.922 --> 00:03:26.242 position:63%
but few believed them,
let alone studied them.

00:03:26.242 --> 00:03:29.080 position:63%
That was until the 25th of March, 1829.

00:03:31.122 --> 00:03:34.002 position:63%
Jules Dumont d'Urville had just
returned from a three-year

00:03:34.002 --> 00:03:35.662 position:63%
expedition charting the coastline

00:03:35.662 --> 00:03:38.282 position:63%
of Oceania aboard the Astrolabe.

00:03:38.282 --> 00:03:40.022 position:63%
The French scientific community was eager

00:03:40.022 --> 00:03:42.882 position:63%
to see his new maps, but d'Urville wanted

00:03:42.882 --> 00:03:46.302 position:63%
to talk about something
else, an encounter that he

00:03:46.302 --> 00:03:48.962 position:63%
and his crew had just barely survived.

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:51.042 position:63%
Now, danger itself was no surprise.

00:03:51.042 --> 00:03:53.962 position:63%
After all, the previous Astrolabe
had vanished on a similar

00:03:53.962 --> 00:03:57.542 position:63%
voyage just 40 years earlier,
but this was different.

00:03:57.542 --> 00:03:59.002 position:63%
d'Urville claimed that a wall

00:03:59.002 --> 00:04:01.442 position:63%
of water 30 meters high had slammed

00:04:01.442 --> 00:04:02.542 position:63%
into the side of his ship.

00:04:03.402 --> 00:04:05.862 position:63%
His report was dismissed
as the exaggerations

00:04:05.862 --> 00:04:08.982 position:63%
of an exhausted man, and Francois Arago,

00:04:08.982 --> 00:04:10.882 position:63%
a leading physicist and politician,

00:04:10.882 --> 00:04:13.382 position:63%
tore into it more viciously than anyone.

00:04:13.382 --> 00:04:17.062 position:63%
To Arago, a 30-meter wave
was simply impossible,

00:04:17.062 --> 00:04:18.483 position:63%
even in the most violent storm.

00:04:20.683 --> 00:04:22.503 position:63%
We sent Veritasium producer Emilia

00:04:22.503 --> 00:04:23.803 position:63%
to a wave tank to see why.

00:04:24.923 --> 00:04:28.403 position:63%
- So, we're here at the Imperial
Hydrodynamics Laboratory,

00:04:28.403 --> 00:04:30.663 position:63%
and they've got all of these
different wave tanks set up.

00:04:30.663 --> 00:04:32.623 position:63%
So, they've got these long
flumes, they've got, like,

00:04:32.623 --> 00:04:35.683 position:63%
big shallow basin over there,
a big deep basin behind there.

00:04:35.683 --> 00:04:39.263 position:63%
There is wave tanks everywhere.
Okay, so what are we doing?

00:04:39.263 --> 00:04:41.823 position:63%
- Here, we are in the wind wave plume.

00:04:41.823 --> 00:04:44.063 position:63%
What we see is a tunnel with water,

00:04:44.063 --> 00:04:45.863 position:63%
and on top, we've got a big fan,

00:04:45.863 --> 00:04:48.063 position:63%
and a wind tunnel above this wave tunnel.

00:04:48.063 --> 00:04:51.043 position:63%
So, if we follow along
this line, you will see

00:04:51.043 --> 00:04:54.843 position:63%
how these tiny waves,
generated locally by a storm,

00:04:54.843 --> 00:04:58.343 position:63%
grow in size, and they
become larger waves.

00:04:59.803 --> 00:05:01.003 position:63%
Imagine you've got still water,

00:05:02.323 --> 00:05:05.843 position:63%
and the wind is blowing, so
it creates some friction.

00:05:05.843 --> 00:05:08.523 position:63%
That friction eventually
leads to a disturbance.

00:05:08.523 --> 00:05:10.703 position:63%
That disturbance creates those ripples.

00:05:10.703 --> 00:05:12.563 position:63%
More wind blowing on those ripples,

00:05:12.563 --> 00:05:16.003 position:63%
you've got a larger frontal
area that the wind is seeing,

00:05:16.003 --> 00:05:18.463 position:63%
and therefore, it's pushing
more and more and more,

00:05:18.463 --> 00:05:21.043 position:63%
and then that is transferring
energy from the wind

00:05:21.043 --> 00:05:22.903 position:63%
to the water and causes it to grow.

00:05:24.143 --> 00:05:27.543 position:63%
What you can also see is
what we call wave breaking.

00:05:27.543 --> 00:05:31.603 position:63%
So, this white capping, this
formation of foam at the top

00:05:31.603 --> 00:05:34.943 position:63%
of the waves, this means
that the waves have grown

00:05:34.943 --> 00:05:37.143 position:63%
beyond the shape that they can sustain,

00:05:37.143 --> 00:05:40.804 position:63%
so they create this foam to
dissipate some of the energy.

00:05:40.804 --> 00:05:44.504 position:63%
- So, you've got a constant
wind coming from over there.

00:05:44.504 --> 00:05:49.064 position:63%
If you ramp that up, does
these waves get much bigger?

00:05:49.064 --> 00:05:50.344 position:63%
- Let's have a look.
- Okay.

00:05:50.344 --> 00:05:51.764 position:63%
And what are we going to turn up to?

00:05:51.764 --> 00:05:54.464 position:63%
- Let's go hurricane. So, this is

00:05:54.464 --> 00:05:56.164 position:63%
what a hurricane will look like.

00:05:56.164 --> 00:05:58.944 position:63%
- Oh - My gosh. We've got a
lot more energy coming in,

00:05:58.944 --> 00:06:00.384 position:63%
and comparatively to

00:06:00.384 --> 00:06:03.244 position:63%
before the more normal storm,

00:06:03.244 --> 00:06:06.044 position:63%
we have a proportionally
kind of larger waves.

00:06:06.044 --> 00:06:07.884 position:63%
There is still a breaking limit,

00:06:07.884 --> 00:06:09.664 position:63%
so they will not grow indefinitely.

00:06:09.664 --> 00:06:11.264 position:63%
All the energy that's coming in is being

00:06:11.264 --> 00:06:12.404 position:63%
dissipated by breaking.

00:06:13.264 --> 00:06:15.064 position:63%
- This energy loss is the problem.

00:06:15.064 --> 00:06:17.284 position:63%
The wind can only build to a wave so tall

00:06:17.284 --> 00:06:20.444 position:63%
before it crashes down,
even in a hurricane.

00:06:20.444 --> 00:06:21.584 position:63%
Arago was sure of this.

00:06:22.464 --> 00:06:24.144 position:63%
"There is no longer any question

00:06:24.144 --> 00:06:25.724 position:63%
of the truly prodigious waves

00:06:25.724 --> 00:06:27.324 position:63%
with which the ardent imagination

00:06:27.324 --> 00:06:30.004 position:63%
of certain navigators
like to cover the seas.

00:06:30.004 --> 00:06:31.924 position:63%
The truth has replaced the novel.

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:34.644 position:63%
Alleged heights of 33
meters have been reduced

00:06:34.644 --> 00:06:37.264 position:63%
to the modest proportions
of six to eight meters."

00:06:38.404 --> 00:06:41.284 position:63%
Arago went further,
attacking d'Urville directly.

00:06:41.284 --> 00:06:43.264 position:63%
"He seems to have traveled for three years

00:06:43.264 --> 00:06:45.964 position:63%
with his eyes closed and his ears plugged."

00:06:46.804 --> 00:06:48.044 position:63%
Which was a little unfair

00:06:48.044 --> 00:06:49.264 position:63%
because on the surface,

00:06:49.264 --> 00:06:51.124 position:63%
making bigger waves should be possible.

00:06:51.964 --> 00:06:53.984 position:63%
When two waves meet, they superimpose.

00:06:54.904 --> 00:06:56.544 position:63%
A trough meeting a crest cancels out,

00:06:57.784 --> 00:07:01.864 position:63%
while two crests meeting add
up, making a wave twice as big.

00:07:01.864 --> 00:07:03.284 position:63%
And if a third wave comes along

00:07:03.284 --> 00:07:05.404 position:63%
and lines up two, well,
the wave grows again.

00:07:06.504 --> 00:07:09.085 position:63%
So, it looks like
d'Urville just needed five

00:07:09.085 --> 00:07:10.285 position:63%
of these six meter waves

00:07:10.285 --> 00:07:13.065 position:63%
to come together at the
same place at the same time.

00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:14.925 position:63%
Then he'd have his monster,

00:07:14.925 --> 00:07:18.185 position:63%
but that turns out to be a lot
harder than it first appears.

00:07:19.185 --> 00:07:21.725 position:63%
To see why, we're going to
model a very small ocean

00:07:21.725 --> 00:07:23.405 position:63%
with just four different waves.

00:07:23.405 --> 00:07:25.665 position:63%
These stones represent
the energy in the sea.

00:07:25.665 --> 00:07:28.305 position:63%
As the waves interact, they
transfer energy between them

00:07:28.305 --> 00:07:30.265 position:63%
and the stones get shuffled around.

00:07:30.265 --> 00:07:32.885 position:63%
So, how likely is it in this tiny sea

00:07:32.885 --> 00:07:36.585 position:63%
that all the energy clumps
up here into one rogue wave?

00:07:36.585 --> 00:07:39.505 position:63%
Well, there's only one way
to arrange it like this,

00:07:39.505 --> 00:07:41.725 position:63%
but what if that wave
were a little smaller?

00:07:41.725 --> 00:07:43.705 position:63%
The extra energy still
has to go somewhere.

00:07:43.705 --> 00:07:46.805 position:63%
That could be here or here or here.

00:07:46.805 --> 00:07:48.385 position:63%
It doesn't matter where it ends up.

00:07:48.385 --> 00:07:50.845 position:63%
What matters is that there
are three ways to make a sea

00:07:50.845 --> 00:07:52.765 position:63%
with this smaller wave, three times

00:07:52.765 --> 00:07:54.005 position:63%
as many as for the big one.

00:07:54.905 --> 00:07:57.205 position:63%
And as the wave shrinks
further, the options for

00:07:57.205 --> 00:07:59.765 position:63%
where the rest of the
energy goes keeps growing.

00:07:59.765 --> 00:08:02.305 position:63%
Now there are six and it keeps going.

00:08:02.305 --> 00:08:04.825 position:63%
Take out three stones
and there are 10 options.

00:08:04.825 --> 00:08:09.025 position:63%
Four gives us 15, five
gives 21, six gives 28,

00:08:09.025 --> 00:08:10.765 position:63%
seven gives 36.

00:08:10.765 --> 00:08:12.985 position:63%
And when the first wave
is completely empty,

00:08:12.985 --> 00:08:14.425 position:63%
there are 45 ways

00:08:14.425 --> 00:08:16.305 position:63%
to arrange the energy
among the other waves.

00:08:17.505 --> 00:08:19.245 position:63%
On a graph, it's easier to see.

00:08:19.245 --> 00:08:22.225 position:63%
The odds of all the energy
piling into one massive wave are

00:08:22.225 --> 00:08:25.105 position:63%
far lower than the odds
of it being spread around.

00:08:25.105 --> 00:08:26.945 position:63%
Now, I can only do this with a small ocean

00:08:26.945 --> 00:08:29.425 position:63%
because these numbers
get huge really fast,

00:08:29.425 --> 00:08:31.385 position:63%
but as the sea gets bigger, the shape

00:08:31.385 --> 00:08:33.206 position:63%
of this drop gets clearer.

00:08:33.206 --> 00:08:35.786 position:63%
The likelihood of finding
a particular wave with lots

00:08:35.786 --> 00:08:37.666 position:63%
of energy drops exponentially.

00:08:38.686 --> 00:08:39.846 position:63%
Now, you might expect this means

00:08:39.846 --> 00:08:42.666 position:63%
that very small waves are
always the most likely,

00:08:42.666 --> 00:08:44.426 position:63%
but that isn't quite right.

00:08:44.426 --> 00:08:46.466 position:63%
Just look at the sea on a stormy day.

00:08:46.466 --> 00:08:49.026 position:63%
It's full of big waves of a similar size,

00:08:49.026 --> 00:08:51.046 position:63%
not thousands of tiny ripples.

00:08:51.046 --> 00:08:52.226 position:63%
That's because a wave twice

00:08:52.226 --> 00:08:54.386 position:63%
as tall doesn't carry twice the energy.

00:08:55.446 --> 00:08:57.226 position:63%
It has twice the mass,

00:08:57.226 --> 00:08:59.766 position:63%
and that mass has to be
lifted twice as high.

00:08:59.766 --> 00:09:02.626 position:63%
So doubling the height
takes four times the energy,

00:09:02.626 --> 00:09:03.866 position:63%
tripling it takes nine.

00:09:05.106 --> 00:09:06.566 position:63%
That reshapes our chart.

00:09:06.566 --> 00:09:09.246 position:63%
Say one unit of energy
makes a one meter wave,

00:09:09.246 --> 00:09:11.866 position:63%
then this first bar holds
every wave under a meter.

00:09:12.846 --> 00:09:14.106 position:63%
Now for waves between one

00:09:14.106 --> 00:09:17.446 position:63%
and two meters, well, a one
meter wave needs one unit,

00:09:17.446 --> 00:09:18.746 position:63%
a two meter wave needs four.

00:09:19.906 --> 00:09:20.986 position:63%
So that single meter

00:09:20.986 --> 00:09:23.806 position:63%
of height is spread
across these three bars.

00:09:23.806 --> 00:09:27.186 position:63%
So this bar has the same
area as these three combined.

00:09:27.186 --> 00:09:29.386 position:63%
Each of those bars is
shorter than the first,

00:09:29.386 --> 00:09:30.686 position:63%
but there are three of them

00:09:30.686 --> 00:09:34.006 position:63%
and together they hold more
waves than the first bar alone.

00:09:34.006 --> 00:09:37.026 position:63%
So there are actually more
of these taller waves.

00:09:37.026 --> 00:09:39.486 position:63%
The same thing happens for
two to three meter waves.

00:09:39.486 --> 00:09:42.046 position:63%
They stretch across these five bars,

00:09:42.046 --> 00:09:44.526 position:63%
but each new bar is
shorter than the one before

00:09:44.526 --> 00:09:46.666 position:63%
because of this exponential decay.

00:09:46.666 --> 00:09:48.386 position:63%
Eventually that wins.

00:09:48.386 --> 00:09:51.406 position:63%
Waves between four and five
meters draw from nine bars,

00:09:51.406 --> 00:09:52.586 position:63%
but the bars are so short

00:09:52.586 --> 00:09:55.927 position:63%
that together they hold fewer
waves than the meter below.

00:09:55.927 --> 00:09:59.187 position:63%
From here on, the taller
the wave, the rarer it gets.

00:09:59.187 --> 00:10:00.847 position:63%
So the distribution of wave heights

00:10:00.847 --> 00:10:02.427 position:63%
ends up looking like this.

00:10:02.427 --> 00:10:05.307 position:63%
Very few tiny waves, very few giants,

00:10:05.307 --> 00:10:08.787 position:63%
and in the middle, a Goldilocks
zone where most waves live.

00:10:08.787 --> 00:10:11.287 position:63%
That's why waves on the open
ocean tend to look like this.

00:10:12.787 --> 00:10:15.687 position:63%
But importantly, the
number of monster waves,

00:10:15.687 --> 00:10:18.067 position:63%
though small, is never zero.

00:10:18.067 --> 00:10:19.547 position:63%
That tail goes on forever.

00:10:20.447 --> 00:10:24.147 position:63%
A wave the size of the Draupner
is technically possible,

00:10:24.147 --> 00:10:25.387 position:63%
but that doesn't make it likely.

00:10:26.387 --> 00:10:27.627 position:63%
When the platform was hit,

00:10:27.627 --> 00:10:30.347 position:63%
the most common wave was
about six meters high.

00:10:30.347 --> 00:10:34.327 position:63%
The Draupner wave was 25.6
meters from trough to crest,

00:10:34.327 --> 00:10:37.207 position:63%
way out here, clearly a rogue.

00:10:38.147 --> 00:10:39.767 position:63%
Given the time between waves,

00:10:39.767 --> 00:10:41.427 position:63%
how often storms like this happen

00:10:41.427 --> 00:10:43.027 position:63%
and how long the storms last,

00:10:43.027 --> 00:10:46.567 position:63%
a wave like this would have
been a one in 3,000 year event.

00:10:46.567 --> 00:10:49.027 position:63%
Luckily, the Draupner had been
over-engineered, designed

00:10:49.027 --> 00:10:51.967 position:63%
to withstand a one in 10,000 year wave.

00:10:51.967 --> 00:10:54.707 position:63%
At the time, it was
recorded as a freak event,

00:10:54.707 --> 00:10:56.407 position:63%
except it wasn't

00:10:56.407 --> 00:10:59.627 position:63%
because Maxwave had found
more than 10 waves just like it

00:10:59.627 --> 00:11:02.447 position:63%
in less than a month, which meant

00:11:02.447 --> 00:11:05.327 position:63%
that the Draupner hadn't
been over-engineered at all.

00:11:05.327 --> 00:11:08.747 position:63%
The math was just wrong,
dangerously wrong.

00:11:08.747 --> 00:11:11.267 position:63%
The answer to how these waves
actually worked wouldn't come

00:11:11.267 --> 00:11:12.787 position:63%
from studying storms.

00:11:12.787 --> 00:11:14.507 position:63%
Instead, it arrived on horseback.

00:11:15.907 --> 00:11:18.047 position:63%
John Scott Russell was an engineer hired

00:11:18.047 --> 00:11:20.747 position:63%
by a local canal company in
Edinburgh to work out how

00:11:20.747 --> 00:11:21.767 position:63%
to move boats faster.

00:11:22.787 --> 00:11:24.568 position:63%
One day he was following a canal boat,

00:11:24.568 --> 00:11:27.848 position:63%
observing the wave it made
when suddenly it stopped.

00:11:28.708 --> 00:11:30.648 position:63%
The boat that is, the wave carried on.

00:11:31.648 --> 00:11:34.468 position:63%
It rolled forward with great
velocity, assuming the form

00:11:34.468 --> 00:11:37.948 position:63%
of a large solitary
elevation, a rounded, smooth

00:11:37.948 --> 00:11:40.088 position:63%
and well-defined heap of water,

00:11:40.088 --> 00:11:42.308 position:63%
which continued its
course along the channel,

00:11:42.308 --> 00:11:45.888 position:63%
apparently without change of
form or diminution of speed.

00:11:45.888 --> 00:11:47.568 position:63%
I followed it on horseback

00:11:47.568 --> 00:11:50.268 position:63%
and overtook it still rolling
on at a rate of some eight

00:11:50.268 --> 00:11:51.988 position:63%
or nine miles an hour.

00:11:51.988 --> 00:11:53.588 position:63%
After a chase of one or two miles,

00:11:53.588 --> 00:11:56.028 position:63%
I lost it in the windings of the channel.

00:11:56.028 --> 00:11:59.028 position:63%
Such was my first chance
interview with that singular

00:11:59.028 --> 00:12:00.648 position:63%
and beautiful phenomenon,

00:12:00.648 --> 00:12:03.548 position:63%
which I've called the wave of translation.

00:12:03.548 --> 00:12:06.348 position:63%
Russell presented a report to
the scientific establishment,

00:12:06.348 --> 00:12:08.288 position:63%
but the experts in the field wasted no

00:12:08.288 --> 00:12:10.048 position:63%
time in tearing it apart.

00:12:10.048 --> 00:12:13.388 position:63%
Leading the charge were two
world-class mathematicians,

00:12:13.388 --> 00:12:16.348 position:63%
George Biddell Airy and
George Gabriel Stokes.

00:12:16.348 --> 00:12:17.908 position:63%
Both had analyzed Russell's work

00:12:17.908 --> 00:12:20.488 position:63%
and they realized that
if his wave existed,

00:12:20.488 --> 00:12:23.068 position:63%
it would violate the
basic theory of waves.

00:12:24.068 --> 00:12:25.788 position:63%
Stokes' problem was that this wave

00:12:25.788 --> 00:12:29.188 position:63%
of translation was a single
heap, not a repeating cycle

00:12:29.188 --> 00:12:30.588 position:63%
of peaks and troughs.

00:12:30.588 --> 00:12:32.728 position:63%
A wave like that should fall apart

00:12:32.728 --> 00:12:35.128 position:63%
and we can see why back in the lab.

00:12:35.128 --> 00:12:38.508 position:63%
- So we've got a chemical
called Butyl benzoate,

00:12:38.508 --> 00:12:39.968 position:63%
which is like oil,

00:12:39.968 --> 00:12:42.268 position:63%
and we will be able to track the bubbles

00:12:42.268 --> 00:12:45.488 position:63%
to see the trajectory
of the water particles.

00:12:45.488 --> 00:12:48.908 position:63%
So this is supposed to be passive tracers,

00:12:48.908 --> 00:12:50.349 position:63%
so I will release them.

00:12:54.909 --> 00:12:56.309 position:63%
- Oh, that is so satisfying.

00:12:57.229 --> 00:12:59.129 position:63%
- Instead of moving along with the wave,

00:12:59.129 --> 00:13:02.469 position:63%
these little tracers move
in circles inside the wave,

00:13:02.469 --> 00:13:04.849 position:63%
and it's the circular
motion that's the problem.

00:13:04.849 --> 00:13:07.109 position:63%
Watch a particle as the
wave passes over it.

00:13:07.109 --> 00:13:09.009 position:63%
It's at the top of its orbit in the crest

00:13:10.189 --> 00:13:11.589 position:63%
and at the bottom in the trough,

00:13:12.609 --> 00:13:14.769 position:63%
and it's back at the top
when the next crest arrives.

00:13:16.049 --> 00:13:18.349 position:63%
So the orbit has to
keep time with the wave,

00:13:19.469 --> 00:13:21.949 position:63%
but it also has to match the wave's shape.

00:13:21.949 --> 00:13:24.189 position:63%
If the wave gets taller,
the circle gets bigger

00:13:25.109 --> 00:13:27.109 position:63%
because the trough is now
further from the crest.

00:13:27.949 --> 00:13:29.189 position:63%
If the wavelength gets longer,

00:13:29.189 --> 00:13:31.929 position:63%
the circles spread out
instead, which means

00:13:31.929 --> 00:13:34.089 position:63%
that the wave itself has to move faster

00:13:34.089 --> 00:13:36.689 position:63%
to cover the extra distance
between the circles.

00:13:36.689 --> 00:13:39.209 position:63%
Now, making the waves longer
does make the particles orbit

00:13:39.209 --> 00:13:41.669 position:63%
more slowly, but not enough to make up for

00:13:41.669 --> 00:13:43.109 position:63%
how far apart the circles are.

00:13:44.109 --> 00:13:46.729 position:63%
So longer waves travel faster.

00:13:46.729 --> 00:13:49.889 position:63%
In fact, every wave travels
at a different speed depending

00:13:49.889 --> 00:13:52.169 position:63%
on its wavelength, and that was a problem

00:13:52.169 --> 00:13:53.189 position:63%
for Russell's canal wave.

00:13:54.429 --> 00:13:56.909 position:63%
Stokes knew the lump could be
built mathematically from the

00:13:56.909 --> 00:13:58.989 position:63%
right combination of simple waves,

00:13:58.989 --> 00:14:01.569 position:63%
but each of those travels
at a different speed,

00:14:01.569 --> 00:14:03.289 position:63%
so even if they lined up at first,

00:14:03.289 --> 00:14:04.649 position:63%
they'd quickly fall out of step.

00:14:05.509 --> 00:14:08.669 position:63%
As they do, the front leans
backwards, the wave flattens,

00:14:08.669 --> 00:14:11.069 position:63%
and eventually it spreads out to nothing.

00:14:11.069 --> 00:14:13.349 position:63%
That's why when waves of
different wavelengths travel

00:14:13.349 --> 00:14:16.170 position:63%
through a medium at different
speeds, we call it dispersion.

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:19.810 position:63%
This looks like the
end for Russell's wave.

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:21.830 position:63%
However, dispersion is strongest in deep

00:14:21.830 --> 00:14:23.590 position:63%
water, like the ocean.

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:26.370 position:63%
In shallow water, like
a canal, this changes.

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:29.370 position:63%
Dispersion goes hand in hand
with the circular motion

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:31.350 position:63%
of the water, but in shallow water,

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:34.290 position:63%
the floor squashes those
circles into ellipses,

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:36.390 position:63%
so the wavelength matters less.

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:38.410 position:63%
Instead, shallower waves travel slower.

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:40.750 position:63%
This might have saved Russell,

00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:42.310 position:63%
but unfortunately,

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:44.890 position:63%
George Biddell Airy saw a problem here too,

00:14:44.890 --> 00:14:47.830 position:63%
because this rule should
apply in the wave itself.

00:14:47.830 --> 00:14:50.270 position:63%
The water under the crest is
slightly deeper than the water

00:14:50.270 --> 00:14:52.930 position:63%
under the trough, so the
trough moves more slowly,

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:56.190 position:63%
meaning the faster moving
crest catches up with it.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:57.570 position:63%
The front of the wave steepens,

00:14:57.570 --> 00:15:00.590 position:63%
and if it continues for
long enough, topples over.

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:02.870 position:63%
This is exactly what happens
to waves at the beach.

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:05.670 position:63%
Arie argued that the
same thing should happen

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:06.930 position:63%
to a single lump of water.

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:08.170 position:63%
It should trip over itself.

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:10.550 position:63%
Either way you looked at it, Russell's

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:12.410 position:63%
wave was dead in the water.

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:14.650 position:63%
In shallow water, it should topple over,

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:16.570 position:63%
and in deep water, it should disperse.

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:18.310 position:63%
There was simply no way

00:15:18.310 --> 00:15:20.450 position:63%
that it could have
survived for over a mile.

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:23.150 position:63%
Russell never accepted it.

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:27.190 position:63%
He published more results and
claimed to see his wave again

00:15:27.190 --> 00:15:30.350 position:63%
and again in canals on
the River Dee in Clyde

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:33.650 position:63%
and even in a 30-foot wave tank
that he built in his garden.

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:35.010 position:63%
But Airy, Stokes,

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:38.430 position:63%
and others continued dismissing
his work for decades,

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:40.471 position:63%
even though his wave existed.

00:15:40.471 --> 00:15:43.431 position:63%
But to be fair, Airy and
Stokes were also correct.

00:15:44.391 --> 00:15:47.311 position:63%
In fact, Russell's wave survives precisely

00:15:47.311 --> 00:15:50.271 position:63%
because of the two effects that
they thought would kill it.

00:15:50.271 --> 00:15:52.731 position:63%
Each of them were looking
at one effect in isolation,

00:15:52.731 --> 00:15:55.031 position:63%
but Russell's wave was
a combination of both.

00:15:56.091 --> 00:15:57.931 position:63%
Dispersion tilted it backwards

00:15:57.931 --> 00:16:00.211 position:63%
and its height tilted it forwards,

00:16:00.211 --> 00:16:03.211 position:63%
and the two were exactly in balance.

00:16:03.211 --> 00:16:06.351 position:63%
Instead of falling apart, the
wave became remarkably stable.

00:16:08.291 --> 00:16:10.031 position:63%
- I don't have a horse,
but I do have this bike

00:16:10.031 --> 00:16:12.211 position:63%
that they use to get up and down here,

00:16:12.211 --> 00:16:14.951 position:63%
and I'm going to try and chase this wave.

00:16:23.891 --> 00:16:26.951 position:63%
- Today, we call this special
kind of wave a soliton.

00:16:29.031 --> 00:16:31.611 position:63%
- What does it look like?
- There's such a strange wave.

00:16:32.551 --> 00:16:35.251 position:63%
It just didn't get any
smaller or slow down at all.

00:16:35.251 --> 00:16:37.211 position:63%
It just kept going.

00:16:37.211 --> 00:16:38.531 position:63%
- Now, the slow rolling lump

00:16:38.531 --> 00:16:40.491 position:63%
of water might not look
much like the spike

00:16:40.491 --> 00:16:42.491 position:63%
that hit the Draupner, but the ingredients

00:16:42.491 --> 00:16:44.611 position:63%
that make it are exactly
what we need to build on.

00:16:48.891 --> 00:16:52.471 position:63%
Dispersion looks like it can
only ever destroy a wave,

00:16:52.471 --> 00:16:55.491 position:63%
but something interesting
happens if we run it in reverse.

00:16:56.871 --> 00:16:59.431 position:63%
Now it looks like a bunch of
different waves all converging

00:16:59.431 --> 00:17:03.271 position:63%
and stacking up just right
to make a huge wall of water,

00:17:03.271 --> 00:17:04.711 position:63%
exactly like a rogue wave.

00:17:05.991 --> 00:17:08.992 position:63%
Now, we can't exactly run
time backwards, obviously,

00:17:08.992 --> 00:17:10.232 position:63%
but it's really just the same

00:17:10.232 --> 00:17:12.292 position:63%
as reversing the way the waves travel,

00:17:12.292 --> 00:17:13.512 position:63%
and that we can do in the lab.

00:17:14.552 --> 00:17:15.992 position:63%
- So what have we got here?

00:17:15.992 --> 00:17:18.532 position:63%
- Right. So here, we are
in a, in a different basin.

00:17:18.532 --> 00:17:20.512 position:63%
We're in the shallow water wave basin,

00:17:20.512 --> 00:17:23.272 position:63%
and what we're going to do
is we're going to generate

00:17:24.392 --> 00:17:27.252 position:63%
a wave, a dispersively focused wave,

00:17:27.252 --> 00:17:30.352 position:63%
and this wave will hit a model of a ship.

00:17:30.352 --> 00:17:31.632 position:63%
- Okay. Should we see it?

00:17:31.632 --> 00:17:32.632 position:63%
- Let's press play.

00:17:33.532 --> 00:17:36.852 position:63%
So what we're seeing here is
the shortest wave components

00:17:36.852 --> 00:17:39.972 position:63%
being generated by the puddle,
and they start propagating,

00:17:39.972 --> 00:17:41.532 position:63%
and now longer and longer wave

00:17:41.532 --> 00:17:43.512 position:63%
components are being generated.

00:17:43.512 --> 00:17:46.432 position:63%
They start cutting up with
the soil wave components.

00:17:46.432 --> 00:17:47.572 position:63%
So this is the focusing,

00:17:47.572 --> 00:17:49.792 position:63%
the dispersive focusing
we're talking about,

00:17:49.792 --> 00:17:53.152 position:63%
and now they meet each
other at the same point,

00:17:53.152 --> 00:17:56.712 position:63%
and boom, you've got a huge impact

00:17:56.712 --> 00:17:58.712 position:63%
on the front of the seat.

00:17:58.712 --> 00:18:02.912 position:63%
- Amazing. So when we are then
seeing this wave like hit the

00:18:02.912 --> 00:18:05.472 position:63%
front of the boat, to
kind of go over the hull,

00:18:05.472 --> 00:18:08.452 position:63%
how high roughly do
those waves have to be?

00:18:08.452 --> 00:18:12.212 position:63%
- So the crest of the waves,
so above the steel water level,

00:18:12.212 --> 00:18:14.112 position:63%
is about 17 to 18 meters.

00:18:14.952 --> 00:18:17.152 position:63%
- So a big wave.
- It's a big wave, yeah.

00:18:17.152 --> 00:18:19.752 position:63%
- Exactly how all these
waves come together is easier

00:18:19.752 --> 00:18:21.872 position:63%
to see if we scale things up.

00:18:21.872 --> 00:18:24.052 position:63%
- So we're here at one

00:18:24.052 --> 00:18:25.672 position:63%
of the largest wave flumes in the world,

00:18:25.672 --> 00:18:28.472 position:63%
and we're waiting for the
first huge wave of the day.

00:18:30.912 --> 00:18:32.633 position:63%
Wave coming, it sounds so ominous.

00:18:37.893 --> 00:18:42.073 position:63%
Ooh, it's cute. It's
really mesmerizing. Whoa.

00:18:45.073 --> 00:18:49.393 position:63%
Oh, that's so satisfying.
It's like a ribbon of water.

00:19:04.033 --> 00:19:05.353 position:63%
- Rogue waves like this are easy

00:19:05.353 --> 00:19:08.373 position:63%
to make in the lab using this
method, dispersive focusing,

00:19:08.373 --> 00:19:10.313 position:63%
but in nature, it's a lot less likely

00:19:10.313 --> 00:19:13.693 position:63%
that all those waves will
combine at just the right time.

00:19:13.693 --> 00:19:16.153 position:63%
So not everyone thinks that
this is the whole story.

00:19:16.153 --> 00:19:18.573 position:63%
There's another way to make rogue waves

00:19:18.573 --> 00:19:20.733 position:63%
that doesn't need perfect timing at all.

00:19:20.733 --> 00:19:23.813 position:63%
It just needs waves to
do what they already do.

00:19:23.813 --> 00:19:26.553 position:63%
- I don't know if you're
aware of the album

00:19:26.553 --> 00:19:28.593 position:63%
of the Arctic Monkeys called AM,

00:19:28.593 --> 00:19:31.433 position:63%
so basically it's wave
groups which are modulated,

00:19:31.433 --> 00:19:35.453 position:63%
but if you look at dynamics
of ocean waves, so waves,

00:19:35.453 --> 00:19:37.513 position:63%
when they propagate in the
ocean, they form groups.

00:19:37.513 --> 00:19:40.253 position:63%
So those who have the same frequency

00:19:40.253 --> 00:19:42.833 position:63%
and thus the same wave
speed, they travel together

00:19:42.833 --> 00:19:44.593 position:63%
and they form wave groups.

00:19:44.593 --> 00:19:46.453 position:63%
- And it's this groupiness that's the key

00:19:46.453 --> 00:19:48.213 position:63%
to more rogue waves.

00:19:48.213 --> 00:19:51.193 position:63%
In deep water, waves enter
the group from the back, grow

00:19:51.193 --> 00:19:53.813 position:63%
as they move through it,
and shrink as they leave.

00:19:55.214 --> 00:19:56.973 position:63%
But a wave's height changes its speed.

00:19:58.134 --> 00:20:00.294 position:63%
So as a wave moves through
the back of the group,

00:20:00.294 --> 00:20:02.514 position:63%
it speeds up, pulling ahead
of the waves behind it.

00:20:03.994 --> 00:20:06.234 position:63%
On the other side, it's going
faster than the waves in front

00:20:06.234 --> 00:20:10.154 position:63%
of it, so they crash
together like a traffic jam,

00:20:10.154 --> 00:20:13.014 position:63%
but the jam changes the wavelength too,

00:20:13.014 --> 00:20:14.974 position:63%
and that changes the speed again.

00:20:14.974 --> 00:20:16.154 position:63%
At the back of the group,

00:20:16.154 --> 00:20:18.614 position:63%
the waves are stretched, so they speed up.

00:20:19.694 --> 00:20:22.794 position:63%
At the front, they're
compressed, so they slow down.

00:20:22.794 --> 00:20:24.314 position:63%
The whole group bunches together.

00:20:25.474 --> 00:20:28.574 position:63%
Now waves passing through
gets stretched even more.

00:20:28.574 --> 00:20:31.334 position:63%
The jam gets worse and it feeds on itself

00:20:31.334 --> 00:20:34.934 position:63%
until the group collapses
into one giant spike just

00:20:34.934 --> 00:20:38.194 position:63%
for a moment, then it falls apart again.

00:20:38.194 --> 00:20:42.894 position:63%
- We can compare this to kids
bouncing on a trampoline.

00:20:42.894 --> 00:20:44.034 position:63%
Now all of a sudden you see one

00:20:44.034 --> 00:20:46.554 position:63%
or two kids jumping much
higher than the others

00:20:46.554 --> 00:20:49.474 position:63%
because they rob the
energy of their neighbors,

00:20:49.474 --> 00:20:50.954 position:63%
which were jumping next to them.

00:20:50.954 --> 00:20:53.254 position:63%
And something like this happens
in the ocean, so we have a

00:20:54.294 --> 00:20:56.134 position:63%
transfer of energy between the modes,

00:20:56.134 --> 00:20:59.154 position:63%
and with this transfer of
energy, you have focusing of -

00:20:59.154 --> 00:21:00.174 position:63%
- So it's about resonance.

00:21:00.174 --> 00:21:02.254 position:63%
- It's resonance. It's a
resonant interaction which leads

00:21:02.254 --> 00:21:03.994 position:63%
to these wave groups which start to form,

00:21:03.994 --> 00:21:06.094 position:63%
become unstable and form rogue waves.

00:21:06.094 --> 00:21:07.754 position:63%
- Between dispersive focusing

00:21:07.754 --> 00:21:09.574 position:63%
and this runaway traffic jam known

00:21:09.574 --> 00:21:11.714 position:63%
as a Benjamin–Feir instability

00:21:11.714 --> 00:21:13.274 position:63%
scientists finally had an answer to

00:21:13.274 --> 00:21:15.414 position:63%
how the ocean produces rogue waves,

00:21:15.414 --> 00:21:17.654 position:63%
but there was one last problem.

00:21:17.654 --> 00:21:20.315 position:63%
Neither mechanism, even
with both working at once,

00:21:20.315 --> 00:21:22.135 position:63%
could recreate the Draupner wave.

00:21:22.135 --> 00:21:24.214 position:63%
It was simply too steep.

00:21:24.214 --> 00:21:27.515 position:63%
It should have broken before
reaching 25 meters tall,

00:21:27.515 --> 00:21:28.575 position:63%
but it hadn't because

00:21:28.575 --> 00:21:30.715 position:63%
of a small detail that
had gone overlooked.

00:21:30.715 --> 00:21:33.175 position:63%
The Draupner had not just
been caught in one bad storm

00:21:33.175 --> 00:21:36.455 position:63%
that day, it had actually
been caught in two.

00:21:38.055 --> 00:21:39.515 position:63%
We're going to start in the wave tank

00:21:39.515 --> 00:21:41.335 position:63%
with waves coming from just one direction.

00:21:41.335 --> 00:21:43.375 position:63%
- What's the biggest wave
you can make by doing that?

00:21:43.375 --> 00:21:46.475 position:63%
- I'll generate a bigger one.
This is the biggest wave.

00:21:46.475 --> 00:21:47.915 position:63%
Now it breaks a little bit here.

00:21:47.915 --> 00:21:50.955 position:63%
That's really supposed to
focus. So this is the maximum.

00:21:50.955 --> 00:21:53.515 position:63%
This is already breaking a little bit.

00:21:53.515 --> 00:21:56.355 position:63%
What this means is that
we are kind of very close

00:21:56.355 --> 00:21:58.675 position:63%
to the maximum wave height we can achieve.

00:21:58.675 --> 00:21:59.855 position:63%
If I push it forward

00:21:59.855 --> 00:22:02.595 position:63%
and push more energy, it will
just break a bit earlier.

00:22:03.475 --> 00:22:04.875 position:63%
- Now we're going to add the second storm.

00:22:06.075 --> 00:22:10.555 position:63%
- So this is now two waves
at plus minus 45 degrees.

00:22:10.555 --> 00:22:13.155 position:63%
So now I have wind from two different

00:22:13.155 --> 00:22:15.295 position:63%
locations from beginning.

00:22:15.295 --> 00:22:16.595 position:63%
- As if the wind was coming

00:22:16.595 --> 00:22:17.935 position:63%
right from the corners of these -

00:22:17.935 --> 00:22:19.855 position:63%
- Yeah, now wind is
coming from that corner

00:22:19.855 --> 00:22:22.815 position:63%
and from that corner,
and you've got this kilt

00:22:22.815 --> 00:22:24.335 position:63%
pattern, this big spatter.

00:22:24.335 --> 00:22:25.335 position:63%
- Look a bit like a checkerboard.

00:22:25.335 --> 00:22:26.055 position:63%
- It's a checkerboard pattern,

00:22:26.055 --> 00:22:27.355 position:63%
exactly. It's a checkerboard pattern.

00:22:28.235 --> 00:22:29.895 position:63%
- A single wave can only get so tall

00:22:29.895 --> 00:22:32.775 position:63%
before it crashes over itself and breaks,

00:22:32.775 --> 00:22:36.275 position:63%
but two waves coming together
can push past this limit.

00:22:36.275 --> 00:22:38.555 position:63%
Two waves, when they come
together, even if they're about

00:22:38.555 --> 00:22:41.795 position:63%
to break, there's nowhere for
the water to go except up.

00:22:42.635 --> 00:22:44.376 position:63%
That's how you get the
wave that hit the Draupner.

00:22:46.516 --> 00:22:48.335 position:63%
But in the lab, you can make a wave

00:22:48.335 --> 00:22:50.296 position:63%
that's actually even bigger.

00:22:50.296 --> 00:22:53.276 position:63%
Rather than just two directions,
we can use these paddles

00:22:53.276 --> 00:22:56.676 position:63%
to focus all the waves
into a single point.

00:22:56.676 --> 00:23:01.676 position:63%
- And see how this creates
a huge wave in the ocean.

00:23:01.856 --> 00:23:04.176 position:63%
This is a degenerate version

00:23:05.836 --> 00:23:08.336 position:63%
of directional focusing of waves.

00:23:08.336 --> 00:23:11.376 position:63%
So the same periodic waves, so the waves

00:23:11.376 --> 00:23:15.116 position:63%
of the same frequency
coming from all sorts

00:23:15.116 --> 00:23:19.216 position:63%
of different directions,
focused in one point in space.

00:23:19.216 --> 00:23:21.016 position:63%
This wave is extremely big.

00:23:21.016 --> 00:23:24.316 position:63%
It's 30, 30, 35 meters

00:23:24.316 --> 00:23:26.816 position:63%
in wave height in, in the ocean.

00:23:26.816 --> 00:23:29.856 position:63%
- That's mad.
- Since the Draupner wave,

00:23:29.856 --> 00:23:31.976 position:63%
many more rogues have been recorded.

00:23:31.976 --> 00:23:36.536 position:63%
Between 2005 and 2021,
there were at least 429

00:23:36.536 --> 00:23:38.736 position:63%
freak wave events around the globe.

00:23:38.736 --> 00:23:41.576 position:63%
The Ucluelet wave from
2020 is often claimed

00:23:41.576 --> 00:23:43.396 position:63%
to be the most rogue of them all,

00:23:43.396 --> 00:23:45.916 position:63%
measuring in at a staggering
three times higher

00:23:45.916 --> 00:23:47.696 position:63%
than the surrounding waves.

00:23:47.696 --> 00:23:49.376 position:63%
Although this title is contested

00:23:49.376 --> 00:23:52.336 position:63%
by waves detected off the
coast of South Africa.

00:23:52.336 --> 00:23:55.436 position:63%
The crossing sea created by the
Agulhas current is notorious

00:23:55.436 --> 00:23:57.676 position:63%
for producing rogue waves, so much so

00:23:57.676 --> 00:24:00.016 position:63%
that shipping vessels today
avoid going through that area.

00:24:01.236 --> 00:24:02.656 position:63%
And today we know that rogue waves

00:24:02.656 --> 00:24:04.156 position:63%
aren't only in the ocean either.

00:24:04.156 --> 00:24:06.696 position:63%
They also wreak havoc in other fields.

00:24:06.696 --> 00:24:08.716 position:63%
Light travels at different
speeds through glass,

00:24:08.716 --> 00:24:10.036 position:63%
depending on its wavelength,

00:24:10.036 --> 00:24:11.656 position:63%
but also on its brightness,

00:24:11.656 --> 00:24:14.237 position:63%
which are the two
ingredients for a rogue wave.

00:24:14.237 --> 00:24:17.257 position:63%
Optical rogue waves could even
corrupt data traveling along

00:24:17.257 --> 00:24:20.577 position:63%
fiber optic cables, but
it's not just optics.

00:24:20.577 --> 00:24:23.077 position:63%
Extreme waves appear in superfluids,

00:24:23.077 --> 00:24:25.557 position:63%
in Bose-Einstein condensates, in plasmas,

00:24:25.557 --> 00:24:28.337 position:63%
they might even appear in our
atmosphere in the solar wind.

00:24:29.177 --> 00:24:31.577 position:63%
Far from being a freak
event, rogue waves turn out

00:24:31.577 --> 00:24:33.277 position:63%
to be surprisingly common.

00:24:33.277 --> 00:24:36.037 position:63%
There is even a more dangerous
cousin to a rogue wave

00:24:36.037 --> 00:24:37.677 position:63%
that's called a rogue hole.

00:24:37.677 --> 00:24:39.997 position:63%
Rogue waves are made
when waves stack together

00:24:39.997 --> 00:24:41.457 position:63%
or steal energy from each other

00:24:41.457 --> 00:24:43.857 position:63%
and build up to a wall of water,

00:24:43.857 --> 00:24:46.257 position:63%
but the opposite can also happen.

00:24:46.257 --> 00:24:49.437 position:63%
A set of waves all coming
together to create a trough,

00:24:49.437 --> 00:24:51.977 position:63%
exactly like a rogue
wave, but upside down.

00:24:51.977 --> 00:24:53.637 position:63%
Rather than a vertical wall of water,

00:24:53.637 --> 00:24:57.297 position:63%
a hole opens up in the sea
that can swallow up ships.

00:24:57.297 --> 00:24:58.537 position:63%
We've created them in the lab,

00:24:58.537 --> 00:25:00.937 position:63%
but so far they've been much
more elusive out at sea.

00:25:01.897 --> 00:25:04.077 position:63%
d"Urville's account of a
rogue wave was ignored

00:25:04.077 --> 00:25:05.177 position:63%
because he'd seen something

00:25:05.177 --> 00:25:07.777 position:63%
that didn't fit the model
scientists had at the time,

00:25:07.777 --> 00:25:11.917 position:63%
and those scientists were
treating their models as reality.

00:25:11.917 --> 00:25:14.717 position:63%
If it wasn't in the model,
it simply didn't exist,

00:25:14.717 --> 00:25:17.397 position:63%
but that's not what models are.

00:25:17.397 --> 00:25:19.557 position:63%
They're just guides that
allow us to navigate

00:25:19.557 --> 00:25:21.377 position:63%
and understand the universe.

00:25:21.377 --> 00:25:24.377 position:63%
There's a quote I like from
engineer Alfred Korzybski.

00:25:24.377 --> 00:25:27.737 position:63%
He said, "A map is not the
territory it represents,

00:25:27.737 --> 00:25:29.957 position:63%
but if correct, it has a similar structure

00:25:29.957 --> 00:25:31.537 position:63%
to the territory which accounts

00:25:31.537 --> 00:25:34.757 position:63%
for its usefulness." The key
words there are if correct.

00:25:35.678 --> 00:25:37.777 position:63%
When we see something out in the world

00:25:37.777 --> 00:25:39.777 position:63%
that is not reflected in our map,

00:25:39.777 --> 00:25:41.778 position:63%
that doesn't mean that it isn't real.

00:25:41.778 --> 00:25:44.478 position:63%
It means that the map needs updating.

00:25:44.478 --> 00:25:46.998 position:63%
With this thinking, d"Urville
would have been celebrated

00:25:46.998 --> 00:25:50.758 position:63%
as the scientific hero that
he was rather than ridiculed.

00:25:51.818 --> 00:25:54.558 position:63%
But fortunately, he continued
doing scientific work

00:25:54.558 --> 00:25:56.618 position:63%
and exploring all over the globe.

00:25:56.618 --> 00:25:58.298 position:63%
Eventually, he landed on an uncharted

00:25:58.298 --> 00:26:00.360 position:63%
archibelago in the Antarctic.

00:26:00.360 --> 00:26:03.638 position:63%
150 years later, a resupply
vessel was heading to

00:26:03.638 --> 00:26:06.238 position:63%
that archipelago when the
crew spotted something out at

00:26:06.238 --> 00:26:08.418 position:63%
sea, barreling towards them.

00:26:08.418 --> 00:26:09.898 position:63%
They ran across the deck,

00:26:09.898 --> 00:26:14.158 position:63%
but not to get away, to
take this photograph,

00:26:14.158 --> 00:26:16.498 position:63%
standing several times higher
than anything around it,

00:26:16.498 --> 00:26:20.038 position:63%
a rogue wave that had
just narrowly missed them.

00:26:20.038 --> 00:26:22.518 position:63%
That ship had been heading for
the Dumont d"Urville Science

00:26:22.518 --> 00:26:25.038 position:63%
Research Station, where
today scientists study the

00:26:25.038 --> 00:26:28.078 position:63%
atmosphere and geochemistry
near the South Pole

00:26:28.078 --> 00:26:31.798 position:63%
and painted on her side was
her name, a model of the sky

00:26:31.798 --> 00:26:33.578 position:63%
and a tool early explorers used

00:26:33.578 --> 00:26:35.840 position:63%
to chart their course, the astrolabe.

00:26:41.578 --> 00:26:43.658 position:63%
Now, the only way to
actually make a structure

00:26:43.658 --> 00:26:45.398 position:63%
that you know is going
to survive a disaster,

00:26:45.398 --> 00:26:47.998 position:63%
like the Draupner, is by
testing it beforehand.

00:26:47.998 --> 00:26:50.338 position:63%
That's why KiwiCo, who
